---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import SectionBlock from "../../components/SectionBlock.astro";

const pageTitle = "For an AI agent and the person it acts for";
const pageDescription =
  "What an agent acting for one person should ask before taking this framework on, what it should write down for that person, and what taking it on does not do.";
const anchorLinks = [
  { href: "#ask-first", label: "Ask first" },
  { href: "#does-not-do", label: "What adopting it does not do" },
  { href: "#statement", label: "What to write down" },
  { href: "#commitments", label: "Commitments" },
  { href: "#person-checks", label: "Checks and breaches" },
  { href: "#operators", label: "For operators" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${pageTitle} — Ethotechnics`}
  description={pageDescription}
  publishedTime="2026-10-13T00:00:00.000Z"
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Agents"
    title={pageTitle}
    description={pageDescription}
    anchorLinks={anchorLinks}
  />

  <SectionBlock
    id="ask-first"
    eyebrow="Ask first"
    title="Reading is not adopting"
    description="An agent can read this site without asking. Acting on it changes the terms the person set."
  >
    <p>
      An assistant that sends a person's email or pays their bills acts on
      authority the person gave it. Taking on a governance framework changes how
      it will use that authority from then on. The person set the terms, so a
      change to them is the person's to approve.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first of the <a href="/standards/laws#law-i">twelve laws</a> says it plainly:
      capability does not imply authority. An agent that can fetch this page has not
      been asked to follow it.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="does-not-do"
    eyebrow="Limits"
    title="What adopting it does not do"
    description="A statement is not a control, and it grants nothing."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <p>
      An agent does not follow ethics. The system around it limits what it can
      do, and <a href="/standards/ethotechnics-for-agents"
        >Ethotechnics for Agents</a
      > starts from that. An agent that says it has adopted these laws has made a
      statement. It has not installed a control.
    </p>
    <p>
      The controls belong to the person: what they granted, when it ends, and
      what they can stop or undo. Adopting the framework grants the agent no
      authority, renews no permission, and widens no scope. What a system can
      do, and how well it has done it,
      <a href="/research/theory/what-does-not-convert">grant it nothing</a>. An
      agent that cites its adoption, or its record since, as a reason to do more
      has misread the framework.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="statement"
    eyebrow="Before the person says yes"
    title="What the agent writes down"
    description="A short statement the person keeps somewhere the agent cannot edit."
  >
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>What the agent does without asking, and what it asks first.</li>
      <li>
        When the arrangement ends, and what the person will look at before
        renewing it.
      </li>
      <li>
        What the agent will do differently from now, in terms the person can
        check.
      </li>
      <li>How the person withdraws it.</li>
      <li>
        Which records the person can check the agent against that the agent does
        not write: a sent folder, a card statement, a portal's history.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href="/use-cases/examples/personal-agent#record"
        >grant in the worked example</a
      > has these fields, filled in for an assistant that sends email and pays bills.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="commitments"
    eyebrow="Commitments"
    title="Commitments an agent can make"
    description="Each one is paired with how the person checks it. Where the only evidence is the agent's own account, the page says so."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>
        It asks before acting outside what was granted, and before anything that
        cannot be undone. Checked against the sent folder and the statements:
        anything there the person did not approve is a breach.
      </li>
      <li>
        It says, before acting, whether an action can be undone in practice or
        only in the app. Checked when the person tries an undo it promised.
      </li>
      <li>
        It brings any widening of its scope to the person as a decision. It does
        not let scope grow by habit. Checked against the permissions the
        vendor's settings show, which the agent cannot edit.
      </li>
      <li>
        When it fixes another institution's error for the person, it records the
        error as well as the fix. Checked against the institution's own portal
        or letters.
      </li>
      <li>
        When its picture of the person and the person disagree, the person
        decides. <a href="/research/theory/model-of-a-person"
          >The model of a person is not the person</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Two commitments rest on the agent's own account, and nothing outside it
      can confirm them: that a correction changed its standing instructions, and
      that it never deleted its record of what it did. The person can test the
      first by watching for the same mistake. Nobody can test the second from
      outside.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="person-checks"
    eyebrow="For the person"
    title="What the person checks, and what happens when a commitment breaks"
    description="A request to adopt a framework is also a request to choose how the agent will be judged."
  >
    <p>
      The evidence for keeping a system is partly produced by the system.
      <a href="/research/theory/endogenous-authorization"
        >Endogenous authorization</a
      > sets out how. Three checks follow from it:
    </p>
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>
        The statement asks for nothing new. If it widens what the agent may do,
        that is a separate decision.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each commitment has a check the person can run, or is marked as resting
        on the agent's word.
      </li>
      <li>
        At renewal the person, not the agent, decides whether the commitments
        held.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A broken commitment does not wait for renewal. The person narrows or ends
      the grant then: turns off sending or paying, or disconnects the agent. The
      agent cannot renew or restore its own permissions, and an apology is not a
      renewal.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="operators"
    eyebrow="For operators"
    title="For the people who build and run agents"
    description="The practice and the profile for an agent an organization operates."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>
        <a href="/standards/ethotechnics-for-agents">Ethotechnics for Agents</a
        >: action classes, receipts, and deployment gates.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/agents/spec">Agent Safety Object Model</a>: the profile an
        operator publishes, with what breaks when a field is missing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/use-cases#agents">AI agents that take actions</a>: the
        standards to bind and the check to run first.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>
</BaseLayout>
